ce:section.ce:para:
Sertoli cell number in the rat testis reached an adult plateau at 21 day postpartum and remained unchanged thereafter. We selected SOX9 as a biomarker for Sertoli cells and CYP11A1 as a biomarker of Leydig cells for dual immunohistochemistry. Sertoli cell number was used as the reference and Leydig cells number was calculated. As shown in Fig. 2
                        , the ratio of CYP11A1+ Leydig cells to SOX9+ Sertoli cells was significantly increased in the 200 mg/kg BPFL group, indicating that BPF can stimulate Leydig cell proliferation. We further detected Leydig cell proliferation potential by using PCNA as a biomarker for proliferating cells. Immunohistochemical staining of PCNA showed that the PCNA (nuclear) staining was increased in Leydig cells in the 200 mg/kg BPFL groups (Fig. 3
                        ). This indicates that BPFL can increase the proliferation in Leydig cells.

We measured the levels of Leydig cell proteins (CYP17A1 and STAR) and Sertoli cell protein (FSHR) using Western blot. The data showed that BPFL significantly reduced the level of CYP17A1 at 100 and 200 mg/kg (Fig. 5
                        ), which was in parallel with its mRNA change. We also found that BPFL significantly reduced STAR level at 100 and 200 mg/kg (Fig. 5), although its mRNA was unchanged without adjusting to Leydig cell number. We further adjusted them to Leydig cell number and found that BPFL significantly lowered CYP17A1 levels at 100 and 200 mg/kg and STAR levels at 10 mg/kg and higher doses (Fig. 5C). BPFL significantly increased FSHR levels at 100 and 200 mg/kg (Fig. 5B). We further measured the CYP17A1 density in Leydig cells per se using quantitative immunohistochemistry. Data showed that CYP17A1 density was significantly reduced in the 100 and 200 mg/kg BPFL groups (Fig. 6
                        ). This confirms that BPFL can down-regulate the expression of CYP17A1 in the Leydig cells.
